---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const ST = "server/src/iki_server/entities/states.py";
const G = "server/src/iki_server/entities/guards.py";
const PV = "plugins/lots/types/plasmid_verification.yaml";

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "A state is a field, and each state says who sets it" },
  { id: "how", label: "Setting a state, check by check" },
  { id: "example", label: "A plasmid verification's review" },
  { id: "rules", label: "Roles, guards and removed states" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Routes, files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="A state is a field, and each state says who sets it">
    <p>
      A type's workflow is its state fields: <code>type: state</code>, with an <code>initial</code>{" "}
      and its <code>states</code>. A record can have several, one per question: a lot's production
      status beside its QA disposition. Each state says which lab roles may set the field to it
      (<code>set_by</code>), whether it locks the record, needs a reason, needs signatures, where an
      edit returns it (<code>on_edit</code>), whether it holds the record's amount, and its <code
        >guards</code
      >. There's no transition graph in core: which state may follow which is a guard on <code
        >state.from</code
      >. A state is never set by an edit; it's set on its own, as a revision of its own (<Adr
        n={[125, 128]}
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="Setting a state, check by check">
    <p>
      <code>POST /api/v1/entities/{"{ref}"}/states/{"{field}"}</code> calls <Path
        p={ST}
        sym="def set_state("
      />. Under the record's row lock, in order:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>It's a project's record, not a registry entry, and the type has the field.</li>
      <li>The state exists and isn't removed; the revision the client read is current; the field isn't in that state already; the record wasn't finally released (<PageLink to="Locks and corrections" hash="final" />).</li>
      <li>
        If the field's current state was entered by signing, the states it can go to change: a
        return to an earlier signed state needs a reason, and a move to an unsigned one becomes a
        signed reversal (<PageLink to="Electronic signatures" hash="reversals" />).
      </li>
      <li>A state that needs signatures is refused here: its last signature sets it.</li>
      <li>Who: a role in the owning project that may set states, a token allowed to, and one of the state's <code>set_by</code> lab roles (<Path p={ST} sym="def refusal(" />).</li>
      <li>The record's conditions: a request's completed state needs every expected subject covered (<PageLink to="Release and completion" />); open holds block a locking state; then the state's guards.</li>
      <li>The reason, if the state requires one, at most 2,000 characters.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Then <Path p={ST} sym="def write(" /> clears <code>locked_by</code> for its own write, writes a{" "}
      <code>state_changed</code> revision naming the field and both states, sets <code>locked_by</code>{" "}
      again from the new states, and records <code>entity.state_changed</code> in the audit log with
      the states, the revision, the reason and any signatures. Whoever the new state makes it the
      work of is told (<Adr n="0122" />). The app reads each state's options, with why this person
      may not set it, from the record's <code>can</code> (<Path p={ST} sym="def options(" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title="A plasmid verification's review">
    <p>
      <Path p={PV} label="iki/lots.PlasmidVerification" /> is a reviewer's assessment of one plasmid
      prep's sequencing evidence. It uses nearly everything in this group: computed, immutable pins;
      plugin rules read at pinned revisions; a state field with two review flows; guards that keep the
      signers independent; signatures; locks; and reopening (<Adr n="0184" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      Its <code>review</code> state starts at <code>recorded</code>. Under the research flow, the
      assigned reviewer signs it off (<em>verification</em>). Under the regulated QC flow, the analyst
      submits (signing <em>authorship</em>), the assigned, independent reviewer reviews (signing{" "}
      <em>review</em>), then QA releases (signing <em>approval</em>). Which flow applies is <code
        >review_flow</code
      >, which the verification action records and nobody can change. This is the released state, as
      the file says today:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={PV} start="^      released:" note="the review field's last state" />
    <p>
      <code>qc_release</code> says which state it follows: Reviewed, itself entered from Submitted.{" "}
      <code>independent_qa</code> is four-eyes written as a guard: the person releasing may not be the
      creator (<code>history.review[0].by</code>), the submitting analyst, the reviewer, or anyone who
      edited it while Recorded. There's no four-eyes feature in core; the plugin says it in CEL.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every signed state locks the record. Because each is entered by signing, reopening one is a
      signed reversal back to Recorded, by someone who holds a lab role that could have given the
      signature it reverses (QA, to reverse a release) and may set Recorded, with a reason; every edit after that needs a reason too. How that's signed is
      on <PageLink to="Electronic signatures" hash="reversals" />; how the lock is held, on <PageLink
        to="Locks and corrections"
      />. And one of its save rules, which runs only when its <code>when</code> holds:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={PV} start="^  explain_alignment_override:" />
    <p>
      It reads the alignment at the revision the record pinned (<code>reference_revisions</code>) and
      runs again before each signature (<code>recheck_before_signing</code>). The pins are why the
      record can be trusted later: <code>plasmid_revision</code> and <code>alignment_revision</code>{" "}
      are <code>computed</code> (only a run may write them) and <code>immutable</code> (refused once
      set), so a later edit to the plasmid can't change what this verification says it examined.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="Roles, guards and removed states">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Lab roles gate states" open>
        <p>
          Plugins declare lab roles; a project's Admin grants them on a membership, or a team's, as{" "}
          <code>namespace/plugin.role</code>. A grant counts only while the holder has a role in the
          project and an installed plugin declares it. A state's <code>set_by</code> names the lab
          roles that may set it, any one of them; guards can test <code>actor.roles</code> (<Adr
            n={[116, 125]}
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/permissions.py" sym="def lab_roles(" />). A token
          limited to some projects has no lab roles elsewhere, and a service account acting for a
          person uses only the lab roles both hold (<Adr n="0210" />, <PageLink to="Tokens and delegation" />).
          How project roles work is on <PageLink to="Projects and roles" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Guards">
        <p>
          A guard is a CEL condition on entering a state, type-checked when the plugin is validated
          and evaluated by iki-wasm on the server and in browsers. It reads <code>actor</code> (the
          person, the agent acting for them, their lab roles, whether it's a service account),{" "}
          <code>record</code>, <code>state</code> (field, from, to), <code>history</code> and{" "}
          <code>last</code> (each state field's changes, with who, which agent, when and why), <code
            >edits</code
          > (who set which value fields since the record last entered a state), <code>holds</code>,{" "}
          <code>draws</code>, <code>covered_subjects</code>, <code>signatures</code>, and the time
          (<Path p={G} sym="def variables_of(" />). Every guard must be true; one that's false
          refuses with its message, and one that can't be worked out refuses too: guards fail closed.
          They run after <code>set_by</code>, under the row lock, and again at each signature, with
          the signer acting and the signatures given so far (<Adr n="0128" />, <Path
            p={G}
            sym="def refusals("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Holds block locking states">
        <p>
          A record held for review by a rule can't be set to a state that locks it until its holds
          are reviewed, so a lot isn't released while a result is being investigated (<Adr
            n="0127"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/holds.py" sym="def blocks_locking(" />). Holds
          themselves are on <PageLink to="Saving and validation" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="A plugin can't strand records in a state it removes">
        <p>
          A plugin that drops a state keeps it as <code>removed: true</code> with <code>moved_to</code>.
          Its migration moves the records in it, each with a revision by no one, via the system, and
          sets every record's <code>locked_by</code> from the states that lock now. The reverse
          migration moves them back (<Adr n="0121" />, <Path
            p="server/src/iki_server/plugins/operations.py"
            sym="class SyncStates"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Assignments and held amounts">
        <p>
          A person field with an <code>assignment</code> puts the record on that person's My work
          while its state field is in the states it names (<Adr n="0122" />). A state with <code
            >holds_amount</code
          > keeps any of the record's amount from being drawn: <PageLink to="Amounts and the ledger" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0118" />'s transitions and <Adr n="0119" />'s <code>not_by</code> are superseded by <Adr n="0125" /> and guards (<Adr n="0128" />).</li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Routes, files and decisions">
    <Routes paths={/^\/api\/v1\/entities\/\{ref\}\/states[^]*$/} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={ST} /></li>
          <li><Path p={G} /></li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/org/lab_roles.py" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0116" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0121" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0122" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0125" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0128" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0184" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
